    "fx.field_to": "To",
    "fx.flow_intro": "A project priced in one currency and paid in another is normal, and so is being asked a year later why a number was what it was. The answer has to name the rates it used, which is why every set is kept as it was recorded rather than overwritten.",
    "fx.flow_links": "Purchasing power is offered beside the market rate and answers a different question: what an amount buys somewhere else, not what it exchanges for. Compare countries with it, never settle an invoice with it.",
    "fx.flow_connects": "Connects with:",
    "fx.flow_step1": "Rates arrive as sets: the quotes of one base currency on one day, from one source. A set is never edited in place, so two figures converted on different days can both be explained.",
    "fx.flow_step2": "Lock a set you intend to rely on. Locking is what stops the next refresh rewriting it, and it is the only thing that makes pinning worth doing.",
    "fx.flow_step3": "Give the project a policy: which currency it estimates in, buys in and reports in, and how old the rates behind it may be. A project without one is not broken, it simply follows the platform defaults.",
    "fx.flow_step4": "Convert. The figure comes back with the set that produced it named beside it, and a pair the rates cannot price returns no figure at all rather than a plausible wrong one.",
    "fx.flow_title": "How a converted figure stays explainable",
