    "einvoice_clearance.identifier_required": "A clearance country returns this, and the invoice is not valid without it.",
    "einvoice_clearance.identifier_label": "Authority identifier",
    "einvoice_clearance.flow_title": "How clearance works, and what it records",
    "einvoice_clearance.flow_step4": "Send it, and whatever comes back is stored as it came. An acceptance means three different things depending on the country: cleared by an authority, reported to one, or simply delivered to the buyer over a network with no authority in it.",
    "einvoice_clearance.flow_step3": "Check it against the country rules first. That is a dry run and sends nothing, so a missing national field turns up at your desk rather than three minutes before the month closes.",
    "einvoice_clearance.flow_step2": "An invoice is prepared for that platform, in that country format, with the national fields the format needs and the EN 16931 semantic model has nowhere to put.",
    "einvoice_clearance.flow_step1": "Register the legal entity that issues the invoices with the country platform it issues them through. The registration is the only record of the identity a submission was sent under.",
    "einvoice_clearance.flow_links": "Documents are built by the EN 16931 engine where a country uses a format it covers, and clear against the invoices in Finance. A rejection is kept with the authority code beside it, because that code is what anybody investigating will ask for.",
    "einvoice_clearance.flow_connects": "Connects with:",
    "einvoice_clearance.flow_intro": "In much of the world an invoice is not a private document between two parties. A tax authority sees it, and in many countries it is not legally valid until that authority has answered. This module records what the authority actually answered, in its own words, for every invoice sent to one.",
